[:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "31 July –  4 August 1996"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/718"} "Lake Sidney Lanier, Gainesville, Georgia"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/256169"} "23 from 23 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "There were many luminaries among the field in the women’s K-1 500 at the 1996 Summer Olympics, most notably " [:a {:href "/athletes/9879"} "Birgit Fischer"] " of Germany, who had three Olympic gold medals, two of which (1980 and 1992) were in this event, and 22 World Championship titles, including seven in the K-1 500. Yet it was Hungary’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10026"} "Rita Kőbán"] ", the 1992 Olympic runner-up, who was the reigning World Champion. In the opening heats, however, Kőbán was defeated by Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10146"} "Josefa Idem"] ", the 1990 World Champion, who set an Olympic record, while Fisher was third behind Austria’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9050"} "Uschi Profanter"] " and Canada’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9226"} "Caroline Brunet"] ", the latter of whom was the 1995 World runner-up in both the K-1 200 and 500. 1989 and 1991 World Champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/9860"} "Katrin Borchert"] ", formerly of Germany but now competing for Australia, took the third heat. Idem bested both Brunet and Fischer in the semi-finals, while Profanter was third in another heat won by Kőbán in Olympic record time. Borchert, meanwhile, survived to the finals only by being the faster of the two fifth-place finishers. Brunet and Kőbán led the pack and battled the entire course, with the latter winning gold in Olympic record time by a margin of 0.236 seconds, leaving the Canadian with silver. Idem, meanwhile, came in for bronze, leaving Fischer without a medal in the K-1 500 for the first time in four Olympics."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Numb